            {
              "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
              "Text": "These plots (working left to right and top to bottom) show:\n\n* The background colour shows the total head count while the two lines show the age of the stock in each group. Because the sheep are bought at 12 months and culled for age at 36 months (see the ManageStock component) there are two groups of sheep. Stock automatically manages groups so when you report characteristics of a particular group through a simulation you cannot guarantee that you are reporting about the same animals. Here when the sheep in group 1 are sold at 36 months of age at the end of 1993, the sheep previously in group 2 (the sheep bought at 12 months of age at the start of 1993) then become group 1. This is a point to be wary of when reporting from more complex stock management simulations. Tags (see the main documentation) can assist here.\n* This plot shows the liveweight of the two groups against a background of their location.\n* The yellow area shows the biomass of the wheat in the ungrazed paddock while the lines show the biomass of the grazed wheat and (on the right axis) the daily intake of the sheep while on the wheat crop.\n* The yellow area in the background shows the grain yield of the wheat in the ungrazed paddock while the line shows the yield of the grazed wheat. The background shows the location of the sheep. Note that there was no yield in 1995.\n* Feed intake per head per day and type of feed.\n* Remaining supplement stores against a background of the location of the sheep.\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "Name": "Memo1",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [],
              "Enabled": true,
              "ReadOnly": false
            }
          ],
          "Enabled": true,
          "ReadOnly": false
        }
      ],
      "Enabled": true,
      "ReadOnly": false
    },
    {
      "$type": "Models.Core.Folder, Models",
      "ShowInDocs": false,
      "Name": "Changing Stock Genetic Parameters",
      "ResourceName": null,
      "Children": [
        {
          "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
          "Text": "# Changing Stock Genetic Parameters\r\n\r\n## Overview\n\nThe Stock model is an implementation of the CSIRO’s Australian Feeding Standards as expressed in the Grazplan (https://grazplan.csiro.au/) model.\r\n\r\nThe technical paper describing Grazplan can be found at https://grazplan.csiro.au/wp-content/uploads/2007/08/TechPaperMay12.pdf\n\nThe structure of the stock parameter strings (which can be found after adding a \"Genotype\" model to the \"Stock\" node) is not user friendly but they are needed so the code stays aligned with Grazplan.\n\nNote that changing default values may have unintended and drastic consequences on other animal growth and production processes.\r\n\r\nA useful resource to decipher the stock parameter strings are the Sheep and Cattle Explorer Excel spreadsheets. The Explorers are also useful to explore the effect of changing parameter values and they can be found at:\r\n\r\n* https://grazplan.csiro.au/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/SheepExplorer.xlsx for sheep and\r\n* https://grazplan.csiro.au/wp-content/uploads/2007/08/CattleExplorer.xlsx for cattle\r\n\n## Example\n\r\nAn example on how to change a parameter value is shown below. In the example, the Growth rate constant (CN1), which controls growth (and hence potential intake) will be changed from its default of 0.0115 to 0.02. There are two simulations in this file - one with the default value (\"Default\") and the changed value (\"Changed\"). The resultant differences are graphed in the \"Difference graph\", in this case milk solids have been plotted.\r\n\r\n1. in the Cattle Explorer Excel sheet, go to the \"Pot.Intake\" worksheet and confirm that the default Growth rate constant (CN1) is 0.0115 (cell B6). Note also any other numbers listed above or below the desired parameter - this is to double check you have the right value in the Stock GUI\r\n\r\n2. in the Genotype node (the \"Friesian\" node under the main Stock node; this is where the animal's default parameters are defined) look for the appropriate name in the left hand column i.e. Growth\r\n3. this name is followed by notation (\"Growth C c-n-\") in which the last two characters match that in the Explorer (CN)\r\n4. ensure that the default value (0.0115) in the Cattle Explorer is found in the array in the right hand column along with the other numbers next to the default value of interest. This is just to ensure that you are dealing with the right parameter value\n5. double click the array and change the default value to the new, desired value (0.02)\n6. click away to save the changed value\n7. run the simulations and note the difference(s)\r\n",
          "Name": "Changing Stock Parameters",
          "ResourceName": null,
          "Children": [],
          "Enabled": true,
          "ReadOnly": false
        },
        {
          "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
          "Text": "\n## A note about how the feedlot is used in these simulations\n\nThe Feedlot simulation is a simple illustration of the Stock model by using animals entering a feedlot, being fed and milked and leaving the feedlot. In addition to the Stock model itself and the Supplement specification GUI, at its core is the ChangeLactatingCowNumber GUI, the Stock operations and the Supplement operations. Both the operation files are based on stock and pasture/supplement data from the LUDF dataset (see the Validation memo for details). In the Stock operations, data on actual LUDF weekly changes in cow number have been collated from 1/08/2004 to 30/06/2017. Positive numbers denote lactating cows have been bought and moved to the feedlot while negative numbers are when cows are dried off and removed from the feedlot. It was assumed that cows were bought at weekly intervals. For the Supplement operations, six types of supplements labelled \"silage_11me\", \"pasture_11.5me\", \"pasture_12me\", \"pasture_12.5me\", \"pasture_13me\", and \"pasture_13.5me\" are bought. An excess amount of each supplement is bought (enough to last longer than the simulation) and their characteristics must be specified in the Supplement node. The LUDF dataseet is based on weekly reports - the daily pasture and supplements supplied (and their characteristics such as ME content) listed in the Supplement operations was extrapolated from the weekly data to the intervening days. ",
          "Name": "Feedlot memo",
          "ResourceName": null,
          "Children": [],
          "Enabled": true,
          "ReadOnly": false
        },
        {
