    {
      "$type": "Models.Core.Folder, Models",
      "ShowInDocs": false,
      "Name": "Using Tags to Manage Stock",
      "ResourceName": null,
      "Children": [
        {
          "$type": "Models.Core.Simulation, Models",
          "Descriptors": null,
          "Name": "UsingTagsExample",
          "ResourceName": null,
          "Children": [
            {
              "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
              "Text": "##Using tages to manage stock\n\nThis is a simple example of managing animals by tag.\n\nIn this example, 4-month old weaned merino lambs are bought every year on 1 January and retained for 18 months until they are sold. As they are bought, each group is sequentially tagged. Once the simulation is running for more than a year there are therefore two tags of animals at any one time.\n\nThere is a Feedlot and a Pasture paddock in the simulation. All animals can be on the Feedlot and when they are there they can have 0.75 kg DM /head /day of crushed wheat. Animals with a tag value of 3 (i.e. bought at the beginning of the third year of the simulation) also have access to Pasture when the biomass in the pasture is between 1500 and 2800 kg DM /ha. \n\nFor the first two years (before Tag 3 animals are bought) Pasture is cut every couple of weeks. After that pasture biomass is removed by grazing (Tag 3 animals only) only. \n\nSupplement stores are topped up once a year only. The rate of decline in the stored amount is related to the number of animals on the Feedlot (as opposed to pasture)\n\nThe first plot shows the increasing age of the tagged groups of animals with Tag = 3 highlighted with markers. The black line shows the pasture being frequently cut in the first two years, grazed starting in 1997 when Tag 3 animals are bought. Late in 1997 the pasture growth rate exceeds the intake by Tag 3 animals. In the last 6 months of the simulation (Tag 3 have been sold) there is no grazing and no cutting.\n\nThe second plot shows the rate of decline in the stored supplement. The rate of decline in 1995 is low but steady as there is only one group of animals. In the first six months of 1996 the rate declines steeply until the original (Tag 1) animals are sold on 30 June and then the decline is less steep. In 1997 and the first part of 1998 the rate of decline is uneven as the Tag 3 animals move between Pasture and Feedlot. The Pasture biomass is shown as a reference.\n\n",
              "Name": "Memo",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [],
              "Enabled": true,
              "ReadOnly": false
            },
            {
              "$type": "Models.Clock, Models",
              "Start": "1995-01-01T00:00:00",
